  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Lizardfolk are one of my favorite races for a variety of reasons.\n\tMechanically, they have a fun mix of offensive and defensive abilities, and an\n\tinteresting set of ability score increases. In terms of flavor, lizardfolk are\n\tsufficiently alien that they truly feel like a unique race rather than a human\n\twith scales and a tail."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Perhaps the biggest challenge of building a lizardfolk using the original\n\tversion of the Lizardfolk is their ability score increases. Constitution is\n\tfine, but an ability increase in Wisdom doesn’t support many class options.\n\tBecause they need Dexterity to complement their Natural Armor, it’s frequently\n\tbetter to build lizardfolk to emphasize Dexterity over Strength. Unfortunately\n\tthat means that Hungry Jaws will be less useful because the attack is\n\tStrength-based. This means that the race itself is MAD, which is uniquely\n\tfrustrating."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "One advantage of the original version of Hungry Jaws which is easy to\n\toverlook:\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "it’s expended on a hit, but not a miss",
              "href": "https://twitter.com/JeremyECrawford/status/811640192058523648"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". This means that you can attempt to use it repeatedly until you hit, so\n\tattacking repeatedly against high-AC opponents is fine and you never risk\n\tgiving up access to the temporary hit points you gain when you hit."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Both the custom origin rules and the updated version of the Lizardfolk\n\tpublished in Monsters of the Multiverse remove the Lizardolk’s frustrating\n\tability score increases, but amazing they’re no lass MAD because they still\n\tneed Strength for Hungry Jaws and Dexterity for their Natural Armor."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
